From dfcf2945d661556ba936a72b12318b6bbdfc4576 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daemonhorn <dhorn2000@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 10:58:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH docs] qm: document riscv64 guest support

Adds riscv64 guest coverage alongside the existing arm64/aarch64
notes in the architecture overview, firmware (OVMF-only, no SeaBIOS),
machine type (virt), and CPU model (no 'host' under TCG, 'max' used
by default) sections.

Scoped to guest support only: riscv64 is not a supported {pve} host
architecture, so pve-system-requirements.adoc and pve-faq.adoc (which
describe supported host hardware) are intentionally left unchanged.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: daemonhorn <dhorn2000@gmail.com>
---
 qm.adoc | 21 ++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/qm.adoc b/qm.adoc
index 5b46cdc..37f4cdf 100644
--- a/qm.adoc
+++ b/qm.adoc
@@ -47,6 +47,11 @@ emulates 64-bit ARM (aarch64) machines instead. Emulating the same architecture
 fastest options, due to the availability of processor extensions which greatly
 speed up QEMU when the emulated architecture matches the host architecture.
 
+{pve} also supports 64-bit RISC-V (riscv64) guests (`arch: riscv64`) on x86_64
+hosts, using QEMU's software emulation (TCG) rather than hardware-accelerated
+KVM, since there is no RISC-V processor to accelerate against on x86_64
+hardware. Expect emulation-typical performance rather than near-native speed.
+
 NOTE: You may sometimes encounter the term _KVM_ (Kernel-based Virtual Machine).
 It means that QEMU is running with the support of the virtualization processor
 extensions, via the Linux KVM module. In the context of {pve} _QEMU_ and
@@ -139,8 +144,8 @@ snapshots) more intelligently.
 {pve} allows to boot VMs with different firmware and machine types, namely
 xref:qm_bios_and_uefi[SeaBIOS and OVMF]. In most cases you want to switch from
 the default SeaBIOS to OVMF only if you plan to use
-xref:qm_pci_passthrough[PCIe passthrough]. On arm64 hosts OVMF is the default
-and only option, as SeaBIOS is x86-only.
+xref:qm_pci_passthrough[PCIe passthrough]. On arm64 hosts, and for riscv64
+guests, OVMF is the default and only option, as SeaBIOS is x86-only.
 
 [[qm_machine_type]]
 
@@ -157,7 +162,8 @@ Additionally, you can select a xref:qm_pci_viommu[vIOMMU] implementation.
 
 NOTE: The Intel 440FX and Q35 chipsets are specific to x86 guests. On arm64
 hosts, virtual machines use the `virt` machine type, which models a generic,
-UEFI-based ARM machine.
+UEFI-based ARM machine. riscv64 guests use their own, otherwise equivalent
+`virt` machine type.
 
 Machine Version
 +++++++++++++++
@@ -476,7 +482,10 @@ Opteron for AMD.
 NOTE: The 'kvm64' and 'x86-64-v*' CPU models described here are x86-only. On
 arm64 hosts, guests use ARM CPU models: 'host' to expose the physical CPU
 (recommended on the supported hardware), or a generic model such as 'max',
-together with the `virt` machine type.
+together with the `virt` machine type. riscv64 guests use RISC-V CPU models
+the same way, except 'host' is not meaningful under TCG (there is no RISC-V
+host CPU to expose), so 'max' -- which exposes every extension the emulator
+supports -- is used by default instead.
 
 In short:
 
@@ -1140,7 +1149,9 @@ standard setups.
 
 NOTE: SeaBIOS, and thus `bios=seabios`, is available for x86 guests only. On
 arm64 hosts, virtual machines always boot through UEFI, using the ARM build of
-OVMF (AAVMF), so `bios=ovmf` is the only supported setting there.
+OVMF (AAVMF), so `bios=ovmf` is the only supported setting there. riscv64
+guests likewise always boot through UEFI, via OpenSBI and a RISC-V build of
+OVMF, so `bios=ovmf` is the only supported setting for them too.
 
 Some operating systems (such as Windows 11) may require use of an UEFI
 compatible implementation. In such cases, you must use *OVMF* instead,
